From a179b6c452c1e753e3fe79d44cbf351b6cdd0881 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 20:55:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(policy): add an in-app risk engine and task state
 machine

The app relays whatever permission prompts a CLI sends it, so today the only
thing deciding whether an operation is dangerous is the agent itself. This
adds the piece that decides it inside the app:

- core/policy: PolicyOperation (a normalised description of a request),
  PolicyConfig (the user-owned knobs), RiskEngine (pure assess()), and
  CommandInspector, which reads a shell line segment by segment.
- Verdicts are ALLOW / REQUIRE_APPROVAL / DENY. DENY is reserved for static
  rules - --dangerously-skip-permissions, writes to credential files,
  rm -rf /, mkfs, dd of=/dev/..., firmware flashing - and neither a chat
  message, a tool payload nor PolicyConfig.permissive() can turn it into an
  approval.
- Paths are canonicalised before they are judged, so ../../etc/hosts from a
  workspace is /etc/hosts and .. never climbs above /.
- The credential defaults match what the on-device runtimes actually write
  under the PRoot root (auth.json, .gemini, CODEX_HOME=.codex,
  .git-credentials, plus .ssh/.netrc/.npmrc).
- Every reason detail and operation summary goes through SecretRedaction, and
  the bounded InMemoryPolicyAuditLog stores codes plus the redacted summary
  only. SecretRedaction gains one pattern so `--password value` (no `=`) is
  covered, which the engine now needs because it reports command lines.
- core/task: TaskState/TaskFacts/TaskStateMachine. CHECKS_PASSED requires
  checksGreen, READY/READY_TO_SHIP/SHIPPED require approval, terminal states
  have no outgoing transitions.

Limits, stated in docs/POLICY_ENGINE.md rather than glossed over: the
inspector is a pattern matcher, not a shell parser, so obfuscated commands and
scripts on disk are missed; PRoot is not a security boundary; and nothing
calls the engine yet - the ChatViewModel/ClaudePermissionBridge wiring is the
next change, deliberately left out so this one can be reviewed on its own.

Tests: RiskEngineTest, CommandInspectorTest, PolicyAuditLogTest,
TaskStateMachineTest (50 new cases). They are plain JVM JUnit 4, runnable with
:app:testGithubDebugUnitTest; no emulator needed.
