+# Policy & Risk Engine
+
+`core/policy` decides, inside the app, how risky an agent's next operation is and whether it may run,
+ask, or be refused. `core/task` holds the task lifecycle those decisions feed into.
+
+The point of both is the same: the model is not the authority. A tool payload describes what it wants
+to do; plain Kotlin (unit tested, no model, no network, no Android dependency) decides what happens to
+that request. This document covers what exists today, what it deliberately does not cover, and how it
+is meant to be wired in.
+
+## What is implemented
+
+| File | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `PolicyOperation.kt` | Normalised description of a request: kind, guest paths, command line, hosts, file count |
+| `PolicyConfig.kt` | The user-owned knobs: workspace roots, auto-approved levels, bulk threshold, allowlisted hosts, protected/secret path patterns |
+| `RiskEngine.kt` | Pure `assess(operation, config) → RiskAssessment` |
+| `CommandInspector.kt` | Segment-by-segment inspection of a shell line |
+| `PolicyPaths.kt` | Guest path canonicalisation, containment and glob-lite matching |
+| `RiskAssessment.kt` | `PolicyDecision` (`ALLOW` / `REQUIRE_APPROVAL` / `DENY`), reason codes, reasons |
+| `PolicyAuditLog.kt` | Bounded in-memory trail of decisions, with redacted summaries and codes only |
+| `core/task/TaskState.kt` | The task states the UI shows, plus `TaskFacts` |
+| `core/task/TaskStateMachine.kt` | Allowed transitions and the guards on them |
+
+Tests: `RiskEngineTest`, `CommandInspectorTest`, `PolicyAuditLogTest`, `TaskStateMachineTest`.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **`DENY` is final.** It is produced only by static rules (`--dangerously-skip-permissions`,
+  credential writes, `rm -rf /`, `mkfs`, `dd of=/dev/…`, firmware flashing, `policy_override`-kind
+  requests). No chat message, tool payload or config value turns a `DENY` into an approval, which is
+  what `PolicyConfig.permissive()` is deliberately unable to do.
+- **Auto-approval is a whitelist of levels.** The default is `{LOW}`. Widening it is a user action in
+  settings, never a field an agent can write.
+- **Levels aggregate upward.** A request that trips five rules reports the worst level and every
+  reason, sorted worst-first, so the approval sheet leads with what matters.
+- **Paths are canonicalised before they are judged.** `../../etc/hosts` from a workspace is
+  `/etc/hosts`, and `..` never climbs above `/`, so traversal cannot look like an inside write.
+- **Credential paths are grounded in this app.** The defaults cover what the on-device runtimes
+  actually write under the PRoot root: `/root/.config/opencode/auth.json`, `/root/.gemini`,
+  `/root/.codex` (`CODEX_HOME`), `/root/.git-credentials`, plus the usual `.ssh`, `.netrc`, `.npmrc`.
+  Reads are `HIGH` (approval); writes and deletes are `DENY`.
+- **Secrets are redacted where they are stored, not where they are printed.** `RiskEngine` runs every
+  reason detail and every operation summary through `core.security.SecretRedaction`, and the audit log
+  keeps only codes plus that summary.
+- **The trail is bounded.** `InMemoryPolicyAuditLog` drops the oldest entry past 200. It is not
+  tamper-evident and does not survive process death; persisting it is a separate, reviewed change.
+- **Task success needs facts.** `CHECKS_PASSED` requires `TaskFacts.checksGreen`, and `READY` /
+  `READY_TO_SHIP` / `SHIPPED` require `approved`. Terminal states have no outgoing transitions, so a
+  shipped or cancelled task cannot be reported as successful afterwards.
+
+## Command inspection limits (read this before trusting it)
+
+`CommandInspector` is a pattern matcher, not a shell parser:
+
+- It splits on `;`, `&&`, `||`, `|` and newlines without honouring quotes. A separator inside quotes
+  over-splits, which can only produce *more* findings, never fewer.
+- It cannot see through `printf`/base64 obfuscation, a script already on disk, an alias, or a
+  binary whose name says nothing about what it does.
+- It reports what it knows: destructive deletes, privilege escalation, force-push to `main`/`master`,
+  release/publish commands, dependency installation, credential reads and environment dumps,
+  download-piped-into-shell, and non-allowlisted egress.
+
+Treat it as one layer. The sandbox (PRoot — which is *not* a full security boundary) and the user's
+approval remain the other two. A stricter design would run the guest commands under an OS-level
+sandbox with a seccomp/bind-mount policy; that is out of scope here and is not claimed.
+
+## Wiring it in (next change, not in this one)
+
+Nothing calls the engine yet: no UI reads a `RiskAssessment` and no permission request is answered by
+it. That is deliberate — this change is the tested core, so the integration can be reviewed on its own.
+The intended seam is the existing approval path:
+
+1. Where a backend surfaces `core.api.PermissionRequest` (and `ClaudePermissionBridge`'s pending
+   requests), map the tool name and input to a `PolicyOperation`. That mapping is the only place that
+   knows about individual CLIs.
+2. `RiskEngine.assess(operation, configFromSettings)` → `ALLOW` answers the request the way the
+   existing "always" path does, `REQUIRE_APPROVAL` shows the current approval sheet with the level and
+   reasons attached, `DENY` answers with `PermissionResponse.REJECT` and says why.
+3. Record every verdict with `InMemoryPolicyAuditLog` and surface its snapshot next to the task report.
+
+Until step 1 exists in code, treat the engine as tested but not yet enforcing.
+
+## Tests
+
+```bash
+./gradlew :app:testGithubDebugUnitTest --tests 'com.yugahashimoto.andcode.core.policy.*'
+./gradlew :app:testGithubDebugUnitTest --tests 'com.yugahashimoto.andcode.core.task.*'
+./gradlew detekt spotlessCheck
+```
+
+No emulator or device is needed: the whole package is plain JVM Kotlin.
